    {
      "round": 1,
      "version": "0.2.1",
      "parent_version": "0.2.0",
      "rationale": "The most common gate failure was collision (finger hitting the table), and it almost always came after a first grasp and release that left the cube outside the bin, followed by a re-pick that went too low. To get the cube into the bin on the first try, pick_place@0.2.1 averages the object-to-gripper offset over the whole carry instead of using one noisy reading taken at grip time (observation noise is up to 1.5 cm and the bin leaves about 1 cm of clearance). It also holds grip and release for 10 steps instead of 4, so the gripper command lands before the arm moves, even with up to 6 steps of action latency. To stop the table collisions, the grasp height snaps to the task's nominal resting height and never goes below it, so noisy low z readings no longer drive the fingers into the table; the placement check also now uses the mean of 12 raw observations. Dev success (40 seeds, sim only) went from 31/40 = 78% to 35/40 = 88%, with collisions down from 5 to 0 and wrong_placement from 1 to 0; the remaining failures are 3 timeouts and 2 grasp slips.",
